== Foreword
<foreword>
Lead occupies a position in classical Indian metallurgy unlike any other
dhatu. It is the most toxic of the classical metals, the most regulated
in modern medicine, and the most suspect in any contemporary discussion
of bhasma pharmacology. Among heavy-metal preparations, lead-based
formulations have generated the greatest concern from regulatory bodies,
the most legal challenges to Ayurvedic exports, and the strongest case
for caution.

And yet — #emph[the classical tradition handled lead with chemical
sophistication that anticipates modern toxicology.] The 60-puta protocol
(#emph[Shasti Puta Naga Bhasma]) prescribed for lead is more elaborate
than for any other dhatu. The use of #emph[realgar] (arsenic sulfide,
As₂S₃) in deliberate excess is not casual — it is specifically designed
to ensure that all elemental and oxide forms of lead are converted to
#strong[lead sulfide (PbS)], which has dramatically lower
bioavailability than soluble lead compounds. The classical procedure
does not "remove" lead’s toxicity through alchemical magic; it converts
toxic forms (Pb⁰, Pb²⁺ ions, PbO) into a less-soluble form (PbS) whose
absorption from the gut is much lower. #emph[This is sound modern
toxicological practice expressed in classical procedural language.]

The framework’s contribution to lead’s volume is therefore unusually
constrained. Lead has serious modern medical limitations regardless of
preparation quality. Even properly prepared naga-bhasma cannot be used
in most regulatory environments. The volume documents the chemistry, the
protocols, and the genuine pharmacological understanding embedded in the
tradition — while being explicit that #emph[modern medical practice
should not include lead-based preparations except under tightly
controlled research conditions with established analytical oversight.]

This volume requires more careful framing than any other in the
compendium. It is included because the classical tradition included it;
understanding what the tradition did is necessary for any future
research engagement; the chemistry is genuinely interesting. But it is
not a recommendation for clinical use under any contemporary medical
framework.

