== 1. Identity
<identity>
=== 1.1 Substance
<substance>
Lead is the chemical element with symbol Pb (from Latin #emph[plumbum])
and atomic number 82. Among the dhatus, lead has distinctive properties
relevant to its classical use and modern concerns:

- #strong[High density]: 11.34 g/cm³ — second densest of the classical
  dhatus (mercury is 13.5 g/cm³)
- #strong[Low melting point]: 327.5°C — second lowest after tin
  (231.93°C); easily worked
- #strong[Soft and malleable]: Mohs hardness 1.5; can be hammered into
  thin sheets
- #strong[Reactivity]: relatively unreactive in dry air; forms
  protective PbCO₃ layer in moist air; reacts with strong acids
- #strong[Toxicity]: cumulative neurotoxin and developmental toxin;
  affects every organ system; particularly damaging to children’s
  developing brains
- #strong[Solubility-dependent toxicity]: this is critical — soluble
  lead compounds (Pb²⁺ acetate, PbCO₃ in low pH) are highly toxic;
  insoluble lead compounds (PbS, sulfate) have dramatically lower
  bioavailability

Physical properties: - Density: 11.34 g/cm³ at 20°C - Melting point:
327.46°C - Boiling point: 1749°C - Color: silvery-blue when freshly cut,
oxidizing rapidly to dull grey-blue - Hardness: 1.5 Mohs (very soft) -
FCC (face-centered cubic) crystal structure

=== 1.2 Nomenclature
<nomenclature>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] and the #emph[Charaka Samhita], lead’s
Sanskrit names include:

- #strong[Naga] (नाग) — most common technical term; literally "snake,"
  referring to lead’s curling and wave-like behavior when poured molten;
  also etymologically related to its #emph[invisible] corrosion (silent
  damage)
- #strong[Sisa] (सीस) — second most common; older term cognate with
  Indo-European words for lead (Latin #emph[plumbum], English "lead")
- #strong[Yavanesta] (यवनेष्ट) — "favored of the Greeks/foreigners";
  reflects historical lead trade
- #strong[Bhujanga] (भुजंग) — alternate snake reference
- #strong[Kala-loha] (काल-लोह) — "death-metal" or "time-metal,"
  recognizing lead’s fatal potential
- #strong[Vapyam] (वप्यम्) — referring to lead’s role in alchemical
  ranjana (coloring) operations
- #strong[Krishna-vapra] (कृष्ण-वप्र) — "black metal" reference

The naming conventions emphasize lead’s serpentine character and its
dangerous nature. Multiple terms for "death" or "darkness" appear — the
tradition was not under any illusion that lead was benign.

=== 1.3 Graha correspondence
<graha-correspondence>
Lead corresponds to #strong[Shani] (Saturn) in the classical Indian
astrological framework. #emph[This is the same correspondence as iron]
(Volume IV). The unanimous attribution of lead to Shani across primary
sources: - #emph[Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra] (BPHS) Chapter 3 -
#emph[Brihat Samhita] (Varahamihira) on planetary metallurgy -
#emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya] graha-rasa identifications - #emph[Charaka
Samhita] on therapeutic correspondences

#strong[The shared Shani correspondence with iron has structural
significance:]

Both lead and iron are dense, structural, slow-reacting (compared to
copper or mercury), and associated with persistence and time. Western
Hermetic tradition assigns lead to Saturn but iron to Mars; classical
Indian tradition assigns #emph[both] lead and iron to Shani.

This grouping reflects Saturn-quality observable properties: - High
density (lead 11.34, iron 7.87 g/cm³ — both relatively dense) - Slow
reactivity in normal conditions (compared to mercury, copper) -
Structural/persistence association (both used historically for permanent
infrastructure) - Time-related decay patterns (both rust/corrode given
enough time)

The shared correspondence has practical consequence: lead and iron
operations have similar timing-favorability patterns, and lead-iron
combinations are predicted to have minimal graha-pair tension (both
Shani; same-graha self-amplification rather than cross-graha tension).

Within the dhatu/jeeva/moola classification: - #strong[Shani is
dhatu-graha] — the "structural/embodying" class

Friendship pattern of Shani in BPHS naisargika (cross-reference Volume
IV §1.4): - Shani is friend of: Budha (Mercury), Shukra (Venus) - Shani
is enemy of: Surya (Sun), Chandra (Moon), Mangala (Mars) - Shani is
neutral to: Guru (Jupiter), Rahu, Ketu

#strong[Same Shani enmities as iron means same restrictive timing
patterns.] Lead operations are predicted to be unfavorable on
Surya/Chandra/Mangala-ruled nakshatras, just as iron operations are.

=== 1.4 Origins and Sources
<origins-and-sources>
Classical Indian lead sources: - #strong[Galena deposits (PbS)] —
natural lead sulfide ore; the most common lead source globally -
#strong[Cerussite (PbCO₃)] — natural lead carbonate; secondary deposits
- #strong[Aravalli ranges (Rajasthan)] — major lead-zinc-silver deposits
at Jawar, Rajpura-Dariba; mined from antiquity - #strong[Sahyadri ranges
(Maharashtra)] — smaller deposits - #strong[Trade lead] — significant
import from Central Asia and (later) European sources

Indian lead production was historically tied to silver production
(galena often contains silver). The Jawar mines in Rajasthan represent
some of the world’s earliest large-scale silver-lead smelting (~400 BCE
archaeological evidence).

