== 2. The Shodhana–Marana Sequence
<the-shodhanamarana-sequence>
=== 2.1 Shodhana (Purification)
<shodhana-purification>
Per #emph[Rasaratna Samuccaya], lead’s purification follows the standard
quenching sequence with lead-specific modifications:

#strong[Procedure:] 1. #strong[Taila (sesame oil)] — heat lead to
red-hot (~300-400°C, well below melting), quench 7 times 2.
#strong[Takra (buttermilk)] — quench 7 times 3. #strong[Go-mutra (cow’s
urine)] — quench 7 times 4. #strong[Aranala (fermented kanji)] — quench
7 times 5. #strong[Kulattha-kvatha (horse-gram decoction)] — quench 7
times

For lead specifically, an additional vishesha shodhana step uses
#emph[Chitraka-mula-kashaya] (Plumbago zeylanica root decoction) and
similar specific herbal media for thorough purification.

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The five-media quenching cycle removes surface oxides and trace
contaminants. For lead specifically: - #strong[Sesame oil]: organic
medium; prevents excessive surface oxidation - #strong[Buttermilk]:
lactic acid removes surface PbO and PbCO₃ films - #strong[Cow urine]:
alkaline ammoniacal environment; lead forms soluble plumbite complexes
in alkaline conditions - #strong[Kanji]: acidic, removes residual oxide
contamination - #strong[Kulattha]: amino-acid mediated surface
conditioning

The cycling between oxidizing and reducing media is particularly
important for lead because lead readily forms multiple oxidation states
(Pb⁰, Pb²⁺, Pb⁴⁺). The cycling actively works the lead surface, removing
trace impurities of arsenic, antimony, and silver that often accompany
natural lead.

#strong[Critical point from modern characterization] (Rajendran et
al. 2014, PMC4007254): "#emph[Sodhana (purification process) removes
heavy metals other than lead, apart from making it soft and amenable for
trituration.]" The shodhana sequence does NOT remove lead itself — that
would be impossible in this procedure — but it removes the #emph[other
heavy metals] (As, Sb) that contaminate raw lead. This is an important
nuance: shodhana is purification of #emph[other contaminants] rather
than detoxification of lead.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High. Standard procedure across licensed
Ayurvedic pharmacies.

=== 2.2 Jarana (Heating with Bark Powders)
<jarana-heating-with-bark-powders>
#strong[Lead-specific step]: between shodhana and marana, lead undergoes
#emph[jarana] with tamarind and peepal bark powders.

#strong[Procedure:] 1. Purified lead is melted in an open pan (327°C
melting point makes this practical) 2. Tamarind bark powder and peepal
bark powder are added in specific proportions 3. The mixture is stirred
and heated for several hours 4. The product, #emph[Jarita Naga]
(jarana-treated lead), is a yellow-orange powder

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

This step converts metallic lead to #strong[lead oxide (PbO, lead
monoxide, "litharge")]: - Pb (metal, +0) + ½O₂ → PbO (lead oxide, +2) -
The bark powders provide: - Reducing-then-oxidizing environment that
prevents sudden oxidation - Organic matrix that holds lead in dispersed
form - Tannins from bark that complex with lead temporarily

The yellow-orange color of Jarita Naga corresponds to PbO (which is
yellow when fine, orange when coarser). XRD analysis (Rajendran et al.)
confirms PbO as the major crystalline phase in jarita naga.

#strong[The chemical importance]: Jarita Naga is #emph[Pb²⁺ as PbO], a
controlled oxidation state. The metallic lead has been converted to the
+2 form, ready for the subsequent sulfurization step that produces final
naga-bhasma.

=== 2.3 Marana with Realgar (Sulfurization)
<marana-with-realgar-sulfurization>
#strong[This is the critical chemistry that makes naga-bhasma less toxic
than raw lead.]

#strong[Procedure (per Rajendran et al. and standard texts):] 1.
#emph[Jarita Naga] (PbO from previous step) is mixed with
#strong[realgar] (As₂S₃, arsenic sulfide) in excess 2. The mixture is
heated through repeated #emph[arddha-gajaputa] (half-medium-fire) cycles
3. Each puta cycle is followed by trituration and re-formation of cakes
4. #strong[Sixty putas total] for full preparation (#emph[Shasti Puta
Naga Bhasma]) 5. Final product: #strong[lead sulfide nanoparticles (PbS,
galena phase)]

#strong[Modern chemical interpretation:]

The sulfurization reaction: - PbO + As₂S₃ → PbS + As₂O₃ -
(Stoichiometry: 3PbO + As₂S₃ → 3PbS + As₂O₃)

The realgar provides sulfur atoms via thermal decomposition; lead is
sulfurized to PbS; arsenic compounds (As₂O₃, sometimes still toxic) are
partially driven off as vapor or remain as minor residue.

#strong[The critical modern finding]: - PbS has solubility of ~0.86 mg/L
in water at 25°C - PbCO₃ (basic) has solubility ~1.1 mg/L - Pb(NO₃)₂ has
solubility ~520 g/L (massively soluble)

PbS is roughly #strong[600,000× less soluble than soluble lead salts].
This dramatically reduces gut absorption — which is the bioavailability
that determines lead toxicity.

#strong[The 60-puta requirement is chemically explicable]: each puta
cycle: - Drives further conversion of PbO to PbS through the
sulfurization reaction - Reduces particle size through repeated thermal
cycling and trituration - Eliminates residual arsenic as it volatilizes
during high-temperature exposure - Increases monodispersity of the final
PbS nanoparticles

After 60 putas: - Average particle size: ~60-85 nm (Singh et al. 2010,
Sharma 2024) - Crystallite size: ~69 nm - Phase: predominantly PbS
(galena, FCC, lattice constant 5.94 Å) - Morphology: rounded, rod, oval,
cubic, circular per SEM - Pb²⁺ oxidation state confirmed by XPS

#strong[The use of realgar in excess] (Rajendran et al.): "#emph[The
addition of realgar in excess quantity reacts with any unreacted PbO
transforming the same to PbS. This ensures that the intermediates do not
contain elemental lead.]" — This is precise chemistry; the excess
realgar guarantees complete conversion.

#strong[Modern characterization (peer-reviewed):]

