]

#strong[Convergent finding]: traditional naga-bhasma is #strong[lead
sulfide (PbS, galena phase) nanoparticles in the 60-85 nm range], with
characterization consistent across multiple studies. The 60-puta
protocol produces a chemically defined and reproducibly characterizable
final product.

#strong[Reproducibility:] High in the technical sense — modern materials
science can reliably reproduce the chemistry. #emph[However], regulatory
and health concerns have appropriately limited modern preparation
outside research contexts.

=== 2.4 The traditional confirmation tests
<the-traditional-confirmation-tests>
The standard tests apply: #emph[rekhapurnatva], #emph[varitaratva],
#emph[apunarbhava], #emph[niruttha], #emph[nirdhuma], #emph[niswadu],
#emph[nischandratva]. For lead specifically: - #emph[Niruttha]
particularly important — confirms thorough sulfurization (no residual
PbO that would react with mercury) - #emph[Apunarbhava] — confirms PbS
is stable at puta temperatures and won’t revert to PbO + S

These tests effectively distinguish complete-conversion naga-bhasma from
incompletely processed material. The classical QC system was empirically
calibrated to detect whether the sulfurization was complete — which
corresponds directly to the modern toxicological concern.

