== 3. Major Lead Preparations
<major-lead-preparations>
=== 3.1 Naga-Bhasma (Lead Sulfide Nanoparticles)
<naga-bhasma-lead-sulfide-nanoparticles>
#strong[Composition:] PbS (galena phase), 60-85 nm nanocrystalline;
minor residual As₂O₃ from realgar processing \
#strong[Color:] Black to dark grey \
#strong[Particle size:] ~60-85 nm crystalline; ~84.6 nm average particle
\
#strong[Source text:] Rasaratna Samuccaya, Bhaishajya Ratnavali, Rasa
Tarangini

#strong[Therapeutic indications (classical):] - #emph[Prameha] (diabetes
\/ urinary disorders) — primary indication - #emph[Mutra-roga] (urinary
tract conditions) - #emph[Vata-roga] (neurological disorders, ironically
given lead’s neurotoxicity) - #emph[Pleeha] (splenic disorders) -
#emph[Hridroga] (heart disease, in specific combinations) - #emph[Bali]
(strength-promoting), #emph[Vrishya] (vitalizing)

#strong[Modern pharmacology research:]

Lead-bhasma research is sparse compared to other bhasmas because of
toxicity concerns. Available data: - #strong[Histopathological study]
(Singh et al. 2010): no significant organ damage in rats at 6
mg/100g/day for limited duration - #strong[Anti-diabetic activity]: some
preliminary studies, but extensively confounded by lead’s known effects
- #strong[Anti-tumor activity]: preliminary in vitro studies -
#strong[Skin condition treatment]: traditional use for chronic skin
disorders

#strong[Modern medical concerns:]

- #strong[Lead is a cumulative neurotoxin]. There is no "safe" lead
  level for chronic exposure, especially in children
- #strong[Even insoluble PbS has some bioavailability] — gut microbiota
  can mobilize it under certain conditions
- #strong[Quality control varies enormously] — incompletely processed
  naga-bhasma may contain elemental Pb or PbO with much higher toxicity
- #strong[Long-term cumulative dose] is the issue, not single-dose
  toxicity
- #strong[Children, pregnant women, and patients with kidney impairment]
  should never receive lead preparations

#strong[Dosage:] Classical 30-125 mg twice daily; #emph[modern licensed
Ayurvedic practice in India] limits use to specific conditions under
physician supervision with biomarker monitoring (blood lead levels).
#emph[In most jurisdictions outside India, naga-bhasma is not legally
permitted as a supplement.]

=== 3.2 Naga-Sindura
<naga-sindura>
#strong[Composition:] Mercury-mediated lead preparation containing HgS,
PbS, and Pb-Hg-S intermediate phases \
#strong[Use:] Specific therapeutic indications, generally restricted to
chronic conditions

#strong[Note:] This combines lead (Naga/Shani) with mercury
(Parada/Budha) and sulfur (Mangala) — a three-graha integration with
multiple tensions. Modern characterization rare; toxicity concerns
elevated due to dual heavy-metal content.

=== 3.3 Lead in Sacred Alloys
<lead-in-sacred-alloys>
Lead is #strong[NOT typically a component] of canonical panchaloha or
ashtadhatu. Its toxicity and structural softness make it unsuitable for
the structural/cosmological purposes of these alloys. Some regional
variants of panchaloha may include lead at low percentages, but the
canonical recipes used in the framework’s structural validation (Volume
on Sacred Alloys) do not include lead.

Lead’s absence from sacred alloys is itself significant: classical
metallurgists understood that lead’s properties (toxicity, softness, low
melting point) made it inappropriate for ritual murti casting even if it
was used in pharmaceutical preparations. The two domains of metal use —
pharmaceutical and ritual — were treated with distinct selection
criteria.

=== 3.4 Lead in Specific Formulations
<lead-in-specific-formulations>
Lead appears as a component in some specialized rasayanas: -
#strong[Sanjivani Vati] — multi-ingredient formula sometimes including
naga-bhasma - #strong[Various asthivardhaka (bone-strengthening)
formulations] - #strong[Specific kushtha (skin) treatment combinations]

Modern licensed pharmacies have largely moved away from lead-containing
formulations due to regulatory concerns. Where lead is still used, it is
in low-dose preparations under tightly controlled conditions.

