== 4. Framework Timing Predictions
<framework-timing-predictions>
=== 4.1 Methodology
<methodology>
Applying the Atlas computational framework to lead operations: lead is
single-graha (Shani) for the primary substance. Friendship pattern is
identical to iron (Volume IV).

=== 4.2 Pure lead operations (shodhana, jarana, simple bhasma without
realgar)
<pure-lead-operations-shodhana-jarana-simple-bhasma-without-realgar>
Single-graha analysis (Shani-only). Same predictions as iron (Volume IV
§5.2): - #strong[Most favorable]: Aslesha (Budha-ruled, Shani-friend),
Jyeshtha (Budha), Revati (Budha); Bharani (Shukra), Purva Phalguni
(Shukra), Purva Ashadha (Shukra) - #strong[Strong]: Pushya, Anuradha,
Uttara Bhadrapada (Shani own-graha; Dhruva activity-class); Punarvasu,
Vishakha, Purva Bhadrapada (Guru-ruled, neutral) - #strong[Avoid]:
Krittika, Uttara Phalguni, Uttara Ashadha (Surya-ruled — Surya enemy);
Rohini, Hasta, Shravana (Chandra-ruled — Chandra enemy); Mrigashira,
Chitra, Dhanishtha (Mangala-ruled — Mangala enemy)

This same antagonistic friendship pattern (Shani’s three enmities) means
lead operations are predicted to be among the most timing-restrictive in
the dhatu set, alongside iron.

=== 4.3 Lead-realgar operations (sulfurization to PbS)
<lead-realgar-operations-sulfurization-to-pbs>
Two-graha (Shani + sulfur). Sulfur in classical Indian convention is
sometimes assigned to Mangala (fire-quality). If we treat the
sulfurization as Shani + Mangala:

Shani-Mangala is #emph[enemy] both directions. This creates significant
timing tension.

Framework predicts: - #strong[Most favorable]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati
(Budha-ruled — neutral to both grahas); Bharani, Purva Phalguni, Purva
Ashadha (Shukra-ruled — Shukra-Shani friend, Shukra-Mangala neutral) -
#strong[Avoid]: Mangala-ruled nakshatras (amplify the Mangala influence
in conflict with Shani); Surya/Chandra-ruled (enemy of Shani)

The Shani-Mangala enmity makes lead-realgar operations particularly
time-sensitive. Pushya (Shani own-graha) is favorable for Shani aspect
but strong in Shani amplifies the Shani-Mangala conflict — this is a
case where the framework prediction requires nuance: Pushya might
#emph[not] be optimal for sulfurization because the strong Shani
signature emphasizes Shani’s enmities.

This is a derivable prediction worth empirical testing if any
high-quality naga-bhasma production data with timing records exists.

=== 4.4 Lead-mercury-sulfur operations (naga-sindura)
<lead-mercury-sulfur-operations-naga-sindura>
Three-graha (Shani + Budha + Mangala). Shani-Budha is friend;
Shani-Mangala is enemy; Budha-Mangala is enemy.

Framework predicts very restrictive timing — only nakshatras supporting
all three grahas without significant tension would be favorable: -
#strong[Most favorable]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati (Budha-ruled —
supports Budha + Shani-Budha friendship + neutral to Mangala if not
strongly amplifying) - #strong[Acceptable]: Pushya, Anuradha, Uttara
Bhadrapada (Shani own-graha; supports Shani-Budha) - #strong[Avoid]:
Surya/Chandra-ruled (Shani enemy); Mrigashira/Chitra/Dhanishtha (Mangala
— amplifies multiple tensions)

This is among the most timing-restrictive operations in the entire
compendium because of Shani’s three enmities combined with Budha-Mangala
mutual enmity.

=== 4.5 Lead-iron operations (rare but documented in some formulations)
<lead-iron-operations-rare-but-documented-in-some-formulations>
Two-graha (Shani + Shani — same graha amplified). This is
#emph[self-reinforcement] rather than graha-pair tension. Framework
predicts: - #strong[Most favorable]: Pushya, Anuradha, Uttara Bhadrapada
(Shani own-graha; double-Shani makes own-graha nakshatras strongest) -
#strong[Strong]: Aslesha, Jyeshtha, Revati (Budha-friend); Bharani,
Purva Phalguni, Purva Ashadha (Shukra-friend) - #strong[Avoid]:
Surya/Chandra/Mangala-ruled (all three Shani enemies)

Lead-iron operations are predicted to have favorable timing on Shani
own-graha nakshatras precisely because there’s no graha-pair tension to
navigate — just enemy avoidance. This is structurally simpler than
mixed-graha operations.

=== 4.6 Lead in panchaloha/ashtadhatu
<lead-in-panchalohaashtadhatu>
Lead is not a component of canonical panchaloha or ashtadhatu, so the
structural validation analysis (91.7%) does not directly involve lead.
However, the framework’s logic for handling Shani’s three enmities
(developed for the iron component of ashtadhatu) is the same logic that
would apply if lead were added to a multi-metal alloy.

The classical #emph[exclusion] of lead from sacred alloys is itself a
framework-consistent observation: a metal whose graha has three enmities
and whose chemistry is intrinsically toxic creates prohibitive
constraints for ritual-context multi-metal compositions.

