=== 5.4 The empirical convergence finding
<the-empirical-convergence-finding>
Lead shows #emph[strong but qualified] empirical convergence:

Classical claims: - Naga-bhasma is lead in calcined ash form ✓ -
Particles small enough to enter finger furrows ✓ (60-85 nm) - Specific
protocol with realgar required ✓ - 60 putas needed ✓ (modern XRD
confirms increasing crystallinity through cycles) - Resulting product is
safer than raw lead ✓ (PbS bioavailability dramatically lower)

Modern characterization: - Predominantly PbS in galena phase ✓ -
Particle size 60-85 nm ✓ - Pb²⁺ oxidation state confirmed by XPS ✓ -
Histopathologically safe in rats at limited dose for limited duration ✓
(with caveats on extrapolation)

The classical procedure produces what modern toxicology would prescribe
as the #emph[least dangerous] form of lead (dense, insoluble PbS
nanoparticles). The 60-puta requirement makes chemical sense. The
realgar excess makes chemical sense. The classical handling of lead
reflects sophisticated understanding of how to minimize bioavailability.

#emph[This does not justify modern medical use of lead preparations.] It
does establish that the classical tradition handled the substance with
chemical sophistication that modern study can recognize and analyze.

