== 9. Closing Notes
<closing-notes>
Lead is the dhatu where classical Indian metallurgical-pharmaceutical
understanding most clearly anticipated modern toxicology. The 60-puta
protocol with excess realgar specifically converts toxic lead forms
(Pb⁰, PbO, soluble Pb²⁺) to lead sulfide (PbS), whose dramatically lower
bioavailability minimizes (though does not eliminate) toxicity. This is
sound modern toxicological practice expressed in classical procedural
language.

The volume documents this chemistry honestly while being explicit that
#emph[lead-based preparations should not be used in modern clinical
practice except under tightly controlled research conditions with proper
analytical oversight.] The framework’s predictions about lead operations
remain mostly hypothetical because empirical validation faces
substantial regulatory constraints that other dhatus do not.

The Shani correspondence shared between lead and iron means the same
friendship-pattern logic applies. Lead operations are predicted to be
among the most timing-restrictive in the dhatu set, alongside iron, due
to Shani’s three enmities. The classical #emph[exclusion] of lead from
canonical sacred alloys is framework-consistent: a metal whose graha has
three enmities and whose chemistry is intrinsically toxic creates
prohibitive constraints that the tradition correctly recognized.

The chemistry of naga-bhasma is genuinely interesting — perhaps the
cleanest example in the compendium of classical procedural knowledge
anticipating modern science. The medical advisability of lead use is a
different question, with which modern practice has appropriately moved
away from the tradition.

